<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maselekwane Modjadji I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1854">1854</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Modjadji I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maselekwane Modjadji I was the first Rain Queen of the Lobedu people in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, coming to power around 1800. According to tradition she was a daughter of the king, and her accession began a line of queens believed to control the rain, to whom distant rulers sent gifts. She lived in seclusion and is said to have inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/h-rider-haggard">H. Rider Haggard</a>'s novel She.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lobedu tradition traces the royal line to the house of Monomotapa, which ruled the Kalanga people of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> in the 1400s and 1500s. In the late 1500s, the story goes, a princess of that house bore a child to her brother and was banished, fleeing south to what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a>, where over the next two centuries a kingdom grew up in the area now called Bolobedu.</p><p>In the late 18th century, according to the tradition, the ruler Mambo Mokoto was warned by ancestral spirits that his sons were plotting against him, and was told to kill them and take his daughter, Princess Dzungundini, as wife, so that the heir would be a queen with power over the rain. Their first child, a son named Tladi, was strangled as an infant. The second, a daughter born in 1782, was named Maselekwane and also Modjadji, because she was born when the sun was very hot; every later Rain Queen has borne that title.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Maselekwane took the throne in 1800, at the age of eighteen. She lived in complete seclusion deep in the forest, where she carried out secret rituals to make rain, and was seen as an intermediary between her people, the royal ancestors and the rain goddess. Her kingdom was small, but she was revered far beyond it. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swati">Swati</a> kings and many other rulers of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> sent tribute to her for rain. A legend holds that King Shaka spared her in return for rain and sent her black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, though Shaka never in fact went beyond northern Zululand.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She had at least two children, Masalanabo Modjadji II and Princess Leakhali. In 1854 she took her own life by poison, a ritual suicide meant to pass her spirit into the next Rain Queen, to increase that queen's power over the rain and, it was believed, to make her immortal. Masalanabo Modjadji II succeeded her, continuing the line of queens.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maselekwane Modjadji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom was ruled by kings until around 1800, when the first queen, <b>Maselekwane Modjadji I</b>, came to power.</blockquote><a href="/state/lobedu-kingdom">Lobedu Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-29,24&amp;year=1844" aria-label="South Africa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Maselekwane Modjadji I"><image href="/maps/globes/g030s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4552 4926h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4552 4926h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-29,24&amp;year=1844">What was here in 1844? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
